#{[#edb/ref 17592186050995 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088840} :activity/id #uuid "478b73f5-7a8c-56ae-b136-ca2543114338" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088952 :tutorial/content "Given any homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation, we can always transform it into a separable equation through the substitution $y=vx,$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x.$\n\nTo understand why, suppose we have a homogeneous first-order ordinary differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y).\n$$\n\nThen because $f$ is homogeneous of degree zero, we have\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nfor any $t.$ By letting $t=\\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =f(tx,ty) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x,\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =F(\\frac{y}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the function $F$ is defined as $F(v) = f(1,v).$ \n\nNow, letting $v = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ (or equivalently, $y=vx$), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =F(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(vx) & =F(v) \\\\ v+x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is now separable! We can separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =F(v)-v \\\\ \\frac{1}{F(v)-v}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Proof That Homogeneous First-Order Differential Equations Can Always Be Made Separable"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088871 :tutorial/content "Consider the homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{x+y}{x} \\,.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be solved using separation of variables. So, how can we solve it?\n\nGiven any homogeneous differential equation, if we substitute $y=vx$ where $v=v(x)$ is a function of $x,$ then we can transform the differential equation into one that is separable.\n\nDifferentiating the substitution $y=vx,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the original differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{x+vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =1+v \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential equation above is separable! We can solve it by integrating both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ v & =\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  substituting $y=v x$  we obtain the solution to the original differential equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =vx \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}|x|+C)x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}|x|+Cx\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456 :question/id #uuid "34c357f1-505b-530f-8bb4-376eada248a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2301" :question/problem "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" :question/worked-solution "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :question/id #uuid "89b7276c-ef41-5123-ad31-b54753656111" :question/math-academy-id "e-2302" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/12/12aec388c94c8c4d4f2099b98f5a489d110fa3ef0d58e7895d9a425e49f8eb77.png)" :question/worked-solution "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n![](images/c3/c3215663fd1a332f750b8af4049404061e2b1c64726c81572d1a1e76816bb718.png)\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n![](images/7c/7c57d5a7c4003269ba9eacf6240394f508347989a9d75665c7070e31cbdad701.png)\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n![](images/8a/8ad7a31876bc67fa69ff24ed41badcf6531ddf52b2d1847af4b0b75595ab5bcd.png)\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n![](images/7a/7a6577d6ac4f3f1fc100a2767d77509fbe211f7d3e7bc7a2bba027080e7e6ccd.png)\nwhere ![](images/42/4247e7a16afb46979bc6ecfc9ae10dc6c92d0f5f1ff5e1b2a1f412ec86a48a83.png) (with ![](images/9e/9e71f72815a80396d1186bfed1047662e1d507561c7160d1c2d89c2f23421c09.png)) and ![](images/01/012dd077bd0f68f82ae37fa50d493e97bd67ac5ff9f301e48ca29e91b1af16ab.png) \n\nFinally, substituting ![](images/f7/f74fcd854fc4be51cd8eed8f4d0649bcc7cf11089a7aa2a7e7edd8d306693f78.png) and simplifying, we get\n![](images/05/05eae130ba10a3386a1c8621ecc3e3d3ec0bb7af352fb4c928cf8bf2d88c21a3.png)\nwhere ![](images/89/8939a3ee49356a0cc8515cce98d84ecf10279dad9d2f76fac07660d0f99a0d7b.png) Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/18/185e5a72b055183d70fb6d9dbc0227190a8114b80b2dc4193d8f542d5c85c0d7.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Differential Equation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088872 :tutorial/content "If we're given a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand the right-hand side is a **homogeneous function of degree zero**, meaning that it satisfies \n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = f(x,y),\n$$\n\nthen we say that the differential equation is a **homogeneous differential equation**.\n\nFor instance, let's take a look at the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{x^2-y^2}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is homogeneous. Here's why:\n\n- Both the numerator and denominator of the function on the right-hand side are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$\n\n- So, the rational function on the right-hand side is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2 = 0.$\n\n- Since the right-hand side is a homogeneous function of degree $0,$ the differential equation is homogeneous.\n\nBy contrast, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{x^3-y^3}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nis *not* a homogeneous differential equation. Although the function on the right-hand side is homogeneous, its degree is $1,$ not zero.\n\n**Watch out!** In this context, the word homogeneous refers only to the function $f(x,y)$ being a homogeneous function of degree zero. It does *not* mean the same thing as a homogeneous linear differential equation (such as one with zero forcing term). These are completely different uses of the word homogeneous, even though they share the same name.\n\nA differential equation can be homogeneous in this sense without being linear, and a linear differential equation can be homogeneous without being homogeneous in this sense. Always check which definition is being used." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :question/id #uuid "f34de8cd-b650-5926-9f06-8127d4fb3a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-2393" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Differential Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088837}}] :activity/title "Homogeneous First-Order ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087943}] [#edb/ref 17592186048384 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120801} :activity/id #uuid "640c579d-7eea-5b16-9fcb-736f4447b313" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121083 :tutorial/content "If a surface is parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ then the **fundamental vector product** of the surface is defined as\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂u} \\\\ \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nIn particular, if the fundamental vector product of a surface is nonzero at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ then it defines a normal vector to the surface at this point.\n\nLet's find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle \\sqrt{2}\\cos{u}, \\: \\sqrt{2}\\sin{u}, \\: v \\big\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120802}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119707 :question/id #uuid "175c458d-36cc-5f90-b6ee-976e082c30a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8943" :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: v^2, \\: u+v \\big\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\,-2u,\\,4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Fundamental Vector Product of a Parametric Surface"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121084 :tutorial/content "Let $S$ be a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane. If each point on $S$ corresponds to exactly one point in $D,$ then we can calculate the surface area of $S$ as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nFor example, let's calculate the surface area of the parametric surface \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\:u-v, \\: v \\big\\rangle,\n$$\n\nthat corresponds to $0 \\leq u \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}=⟨2u,\\,1,\\,0⟩,\\,{r}_{v}^{'}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,-2u⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-2u,\\,-2u⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2u)^{2}+(-2u)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{u}^{2}+4{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+8{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Surface Areas of Parametric Surfaces"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119708 :question/id #uuid "1b4b69f7-fc4f-57c3-bcaf-9df98e4fc5e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8947" :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx = u, \\quad \ny = v, \\quad \nz = 1 - u - v\n$$\n\nfor $-1 \\leq u \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: 1 - u - v \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120830}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119889 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119709 :question/id #uuid "4b202b4e-d8dc-524c-8cb4-ab0c00eb78cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8958" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e3f2da04e23c9b42ea04cba3ee1d7cc069fef992592f09a70cef367b670f0d.png)\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: u^3-v \\big\\rangle$ for $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{u}^{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-3{u}^{2},\\,1,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{u}^{2})^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral That Represents the Surface Area of a Parametric Surface"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120830 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121095 :tutorial/content "Let $S$ be a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane. We wish to show that the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's consider a partition $P_1 \\times P_2$ of the domain $D,$ where \n\n$$\nP_1 = \\left\\{u_0,u_1,\\ldots, u_m\\right\\},\\qquad P_2 = \\left\\{v_0,v_1,\\ldots, v_n\\right\\},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta u_i = u_{i} - u_{i-1}, \\qquad \\Delta v_j = v_{j} - v_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nThis partition generates a curved grid on the surface, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d31bced1b87e460ec115f97b69a51d21261184b52011e26f32a00487e7cd2d1.png)\n\nNow consider a \"patch\" $S_{ij}$ of the surface that corresponds to the rectangle $R_{ij}$ of the partition. How can we approximate the area of this small patch?\n\nFirst, let's make a bigger picture of $S_{ij}.$\n\n![](images/a1/a125124ac911c9e0557760d9f29ed7ae829f6595be58fec4f2ba39989e51f51b.png)\n\nHere, we assume that the grid curves ${\\color{blue}C_u}$ with equation $\\mathbf{r}(u,v_j)$ and ${\\color{red}C_v}$ with equation $\\mathbf{r}(u_i,v)$ pass through the point $P_{ij}$ with position vector $\\mathbf{r}(u_i,v_j).$\n\nWe now proceed to find a parallelogram whose area is approximately equal to the area of our patch.\n\n- The vector that connects the bottom-left and bottom-right vertices of our patch is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u_{i+1}, v_j) - \\mathbf r (u_i, v_j).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated using a partial derivative, this vector is approximately equal to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_u(u_i,v_j) \\Delta{u}_i,\n$$\n\nwhich is the vector that spans the bottom edge of the parallelogram shown in the diagram.\n\n- Similarly, the vector that connects the bottom-left and top-left vertices of our patch is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u_{i}, v_{j+1}) - \\mathbf r (u_i, v_j).\n$$\n\nThis vector is approximately equal to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_v(u_i,v_j) \\Delta{v}_j,\n$$\n\nwhich is the vector that spans the left edge of the parallelogram shown in the diagram.\n\nAs a result, we can approximate the area of $S_{ij}$ by the area of the parallelogram spanned by these two vectors. The area of such a parallelogram equals the magnitude of the corresponding cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A({S}_{ij}) & \\approx ‖{r}_{u}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})Δ{u}_{i}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})Δ{v}_{j}‖ \\\\  & =‖{r}_{u}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})\\times {r}_{v}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})‖\\,Δ{u}_{i}Δ{v}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the approximations for all patches, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(S) & \\approx {\\sum }_{i=0}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}A({S}_{ij}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=0}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n}‖{r}_{u}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})\\times {r}_{v}^{'}({u}_{i},{v}_{j})‖\\,Δ{u}_{i}Δ{v}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Riemann sum, and the closer the points $P_{ij}$ are to each other, the better our approximation will be. So, in the limit as $m,n\\to\\infty$ (or $\\Delta u_i, \\Delta v_j \\to 0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{u}^{'}\\times {r}_{v}^{'}‖\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"}}] :activity/title "Areas of Parametric Surfaces" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120010}] [#edb/ref 17592186049212 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163021} :activity/id #uuid "b4bce499-336b-56f8-bdde-0648c2a18649" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163300 :tutorial/content "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nThere are two more important properties of this type of line integral that we need to be aware of.\n\n- There is usually more than one way to parametrize a curve. However, line integrals with respect to arc length are *independent of the choice of parametrization*. In other words, we get the same result regardless of which parametrization we choose.\n\nFor example, the paths $C_1$ and $C_2,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩, & 0\\le t<2\\pi  \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}2u,\\,\\operatorname{sin}2u⟩, & 0\\le u<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nboth traverse a circle of radius $1$ once in the counterclockwise direction starting from $(1,0).$ However, since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\n- Line integrals with respect to arc length are also *independent of the orientation* of $C.$ For example, suppose that $C$ is a path along a particular curve traversed from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ Then, $-C$ is a path along the same curve but traversed from $B$ to $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nIndependence of orientation means that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{-C} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- Finally, it's important to mention that all of the properties discussed in this lesson apply to line integrals with respect to arc length over space curves, i.e., line integrals of the form $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d s.$\n\nTo demonstrate point 2 above, consider the line integral\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s, \n\\]\nwhere [math]C[/math] is the line segment from [math]A(0,0)[/math] to [math]B(2,2).[/math] \n\nOne possible parameterization of [math]C[/math] from [math]A[/math] to [math]B[/math] is [math]\\mathbf r = \\langle t, t\\rangle[/math] for [math]t\\in[0,2].[/math] We get [math]x=y=t[/math] on the curve, and [math]\\mathbf r ' = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle[/math] which leads to [math]||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.[/math] Therefore, compuing the line integral gives\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 t\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\end{align*}\nIf we *reverse* the direction of the curve, traversing from [math]B[/math] to [math]A,[/math] we get the parametrization \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(0+2-t) = \\mathbf r(2-t) =   \\langle 2-t,2- t\\rangle,\\quad t \\in[0,2].\n\\]\nNow, we get [math]x=y=2-t, \\mathbf r' = \\langle -1, -1\\rangle[/math] and so [math]||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.[/math] Computing the line integral using this parametrization gives\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 (2-t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\]" :tutorial/title "Independence of Parametrization and Orientation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163020}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162081 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161894 :question/id #uuid "55e8e57f-7f9e-529a-aa1b-24e1d86c4133" :question/math-academy-id "e-9470" :question/problem "![](images/76/765415456d7f33c3558e08e7c4fc01ef3e80961c1e073a210033d91741d78f60.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the arc of a semicircle of radius $4$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $C$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 4.$ Its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=\\pi r=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 4\\pi.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals of Unity"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162082 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161895 :question/id #uuid "fdab3229-66ca-59d3-ac20-8a5b3d3506fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-9471" :question/problem "![](images/84/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/f4/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals Over Piecewise-Smooth Curves"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163017}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161896 :question/id #uuid "4d5a8f9d-7324-547c-ae5e-b49294debc1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9472" :question/problem "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds  = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Independence of Parametrization and Orientation"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163301 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we discuss some properties of line integrals of scalar functions $f(x,y)$ with respect to arc length. Recall that we denote these types of integrals as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is some path in the $xy$-plane. The first property we'll consider is the line integral of **unity**, i.e., $f(x,y) = 1.$ \n\nFirstly, by setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is the path along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(4,3),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3abd267bb4482e5782a61ba2e4c026482d09ba798bc32a0a412022cb8a87ef09.png)\n\nAs it turns out, the line integral with respect to arc length of unity along a path $C$ equals the arc length of $C.$ \n\nIn this case, the arc length of $C$ is given by the length of the hypotenuse of a right triangle. Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{4^2+3^2} = 5.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163302 :tutorial/content "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the **piecewise-smooth** path shown below.\n\n![](images/7f/7fcdcfd4e1b133b4b8f4bfef35991e27f33d8bfb2913610542b7fad31e10de30.png)\n\nWe can show that the line integral along the entire path $C$ equals the sum of the line integrals across the paths $C_1$ and $C_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSo, if $f(x,y)=1,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result naturally extends to line integrals across piecewise-smooth paths comprised of three or more parts. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "Calculating Line Integrals Over Piecewise-Smooth Curves"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163019}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162234}] [#edb/ref 17592186048512 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180467} :activity/id #uuid "92c95980-af12-5349-9581-38b036ed9c61" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179525 :question/id #uuid "cd73b214-d887-5dbb-93f9-b71e16711a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-6304" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =3\\,j+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-3\\,j] \\\\  & =3\\,j+t(2\\,i) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =2\\,i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = e^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{2t+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+3,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180467 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180708 :tutorial/content "Recall that the line integral with respect to arc length of a function $f(x,y)$ along a curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t_a \\leq  t \\leq t_b$ is a parametrization of the curve $C.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will consider the case when the curve $C$ is a line segment. To calculate a line integral along a segment, we first need to parameterize the segment.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to parameterize the segment between the points $A$ and $B$ with position vectors $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4d8d4e764dc4d7b29d5871d989100f2bb13b97aa4d034052037de4599d39e0e.png)\n\nThe vector equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=a+tv,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf v$ is parallel to the line.\n\nWe can express $\\mathbf v$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\mathbf b - \\mathbf a,\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation of the line as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{a} + t (\\mathbf b - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nSince we only want to parameterize the part of the line that lies between $A$ and $B,$ we set the restriction $0\\leq t \\leq 1.$ Therefore, the complete parametrization of the line segment is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{a} + t (\\mathbf b - \\mathbf a), \\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq 1.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- You can check that $t=0$ corresponds to the point $A,$ and $t=1$ corresponds to the point $B.$\n\n- It's important to realize that this parametrization traverses the segment *from* the point $A$ *to* the point $B.$ The orientation of our segment does not affect line integrals with respect to arc length, but it is important for other line integrals that we'll meet later." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180468}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179586 :question/id #uuid "b7d08596-fbe4-5601-a58e-dac90715f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9486" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t[(i+j)-i] \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179587 :question/id #uuid "17ec17a0-11d7-57c1-9e7f-6893ab9e51e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9487" :question/problem "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t[(-\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)-(j-3k)] \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t(-i+\\,j+k) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)k \\\\  & =-i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Line Integrals Along Segments in Three Dimensions"}}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Line Segments" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179853}]}
